.TH IPPFIX 8 "August 2026" "1.0" "System Administration Utilities"
.SH NAME
ippfix \- IPP proxy that repairs print jobs for printers with a limited font
cache.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ippfix
[\fB\-a\fR|\fB\-\-advertise\fR \fIADDRESS\fR]
[\fB\-\-also\-advertise\fR \fIADDRESS\fR]
[\fB\-\-archive\fR \fIDIR\fR]
[\fB\-\-list\fR [\fIURL\fR]]
[\fB\-\-max\-connections\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-idle\-timeout\fR \fISECONDS\fR]
[\fB\-\-require\-tls\fR]
[\fB\-\-convert\-threshold\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-max\-pdf\-bytes\fR \fIMB\fR]
[\fB\-\-all\-formats\fR]
[\fB\-\-fail\-closed\fR]
[\fB\-\-archive\-max\-bytes\fR \fIMB\fR]
[\fB\-\-no\-ipv6\fR]
[\fB\-\-cert\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-\-converter\fR \fIPATH\fR]
[\fB\-\-key\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-\-no\-advertise\fR]
[\fB\-\-no\-convert\fR]
[\fB\-p\fR|\fB\-\-port\fR \fIPORT\fR]
[\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fISECONDS\fR]
[\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR]
\fI[NAME=]URI\fR...

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ippfix
accepts print jobs over IPP, rewrites each PDF so that it contains no embedded
font programs, and forwards it to a real printer. Everything else is relayed
untouched.
.PP
It exists because HP LaserJet Pro printers run a combined PostScript/PDF
interpreter with a fixed per\-page budget for embedded fonts and the glyphs
drawn from them. Exceeding that budget aborts the interpreter: the printer
warms up, reports the job
.BR completed ,
and marks nothing. Sometimes the first few pages emerge and the job then stops.
No error reaches the client and none is shown on the panel. Where the device
records it at all, its event log reports an assertion failure in
.BR fontcache.c .
.PP
The budget covers both the glyphs drawn and the embedded font programs they
come from, and the two trade against each other. On a Color LaserJet Pro MFP
M283fdw, one fully embedded font renders 527 distinct glyphs but not 534; add a
second fully embedded font and the page fails at 300. A font's cost scales with
how many glyphs its embedded program declares rather than being a flat
per\-font constant, so a heavily subsetted face is far cheaper than a complete
one.
.PP
Those figures come from probes embedding complete, unsubsetted fonts. Jobs from
a browser subset aggressively and sit well below them: sampled from real Chrome
output, two subsets declaring 93 and 668 glyphs, with 451 distinct glyphs drawn
between them, printed without trouble. No fixed threshold is therefore safe to
design against \(em whether a document crosses the line depends on its
typefaces, how they were subsetted, and how many distinct characters appear.
.PP
The defect appears in firmware builds years apart and is unlikely to be fixed.
Client\-side changes affect how often it is reached: Chrome 130 through 144
embedded a separate font program for every
.I strike
of a typeface, including one per text colour, which multiplied the cost of an
ordinary page. Chrome 145 removed the colour component and normalises text size
out of the key, so current versions embed far fewer font programs than that era
did.
.PP
.B ippfix
converts glyphs to filled paths using Ghostscript's
.B \-dNoOutputFonts
option. No font program reaches the printer, which makes the failure
structurally impossible rather than merely less likely. Text remains vector, so
the printer's own rasteriser still renders it at full device precision and its
edge enhancement still applies. Rasterising the page instead would commit its
geometry to the device grid before the printer saw it, forfeit the printer's
halftoning, and inflate a small job into tens of megabytes.

.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I [NAME=]URI
A printer to proxy, given as an
.B ipp://
or
.B ipps://
URI. The optional
.I NAME
becomes the queue name, published at
.BI /ipp/ NAME\fR.
Without one, the queue is named
.BR print .
May be repeated to serve several printers from one daemon.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-advertise " \fIADDRESS\fR"
Address that clients should use to reach this server. Autodetected by default,
which is wrong on hosts with several addresses.
.TP
.B \-\-also\-advertise " \fIADDRESS\fR"
Additional address to publish in the DNS\-SD records; may be repeated. By
default the stable, globally scoped IPv6 addresses of the same interface as
.B \-\-advertise
are published alongside it, so dual\-stack clients are offered IPv6 without
extra configuration.
.IP
Only that interface's addresses are used. On a multi\-homed host, publishing
every address the machine happens to have invites clients to try one they
cannot route to, which appears as a long stall rather than a clear failure.
Privacy, deprecated and tentative addresses are excluded because they rotate or
are not valid for new connections, and link\-local addresses are excluded
because they need a scope identifier a DNS\-SD record cannot carry.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-ipv6
Publish only the IPv4 address, for networks where IPv6 exists but is not
routable.
.TP
.B \-\-archive " \fIDIR\fR"
Diagnostic only. Keep a copy of every job exactly as it arrived, before
conversion, together with a short text file recording the queue, job name,
document format and what conversion did.
.IP
This writes the documents users print to disk. It is off by default. The
directory is created mode 0700 and the files 0600, owned by the service
account, but anyone who can read them can read everything printed while it was
enabled. It exists because the failure this proxy works around is silent and
depends on document content, so without the document that provoked it there is
almost nothing to go on. Enable it while chasing a specific problem, and turn
it off again afterwards.
.TP
.B \-\-archive\-max " \fIN\fR"
Keep at most
.I N
archived jobs, deleting the oldest first. Default 50. Bounds the disk cost of a
flag left on by accident.
.TP
.B \-\-cert " \fIFILE\fR"
TLS certificate. Default
.IR /etc/ippfix/ippfix.crt .
.TP
.B \-\-converter " \fIPATH\fR"
Helper that rewrites a PDF read on standard input and writes the result to
standard output. Default
.IR /usr/local/lib/ippfix/defont .
.TP
.B \-\-key " \fIFILE\fR"
TLS private key. Default
.IR /etc/ippfix/ippfix.key .
.TP
.B \-\-list " [\fIURL\fR]"
Print the queues a running instance serves and exit, then stop. Intended for
configuring clients by address rather than by discovery: mDNS is not available
everywhere, and some sites prefer printers pinned by address so that discovery
cannot silently point users somewhere else. Defaults to the instance on this
host. The same listing is served as JSON at
.I /queues.json
and as a table at the daemon's HTTP root.
.TP
.B \-\-max\-connections " \fIN\fR"
Refuse connections beyond
.I N
concurrent ones. Default 64. Without a bound, connections that open and then
say nothing accumulate until the service hits its task limit, after which it
can accept nothing further until restarted.
.TP
.B \-\-idle\-timeout " \fISECONDS\fR"
Drop a connection that stops speaking for this long. Default 30. Applies to
every stage of a request, so a client cannot hold a thread by sending a partial
header and waiting.
.TP
.B \-\-require\-tls
Refuse plaintext IPP and accept only implicit TLS. Off by default, because
clients that discover a printer over DNS-SD commonly choose plaintext and would
otherwise simply fail to print.
.TP
.B \-\-convert\-threshold " \fIN\fR"
Leave a job untouched when its estimated font cost is at or below
.IR N .
Default 2500; 0 converts everything.
.IP
Outlining is expensive. It replaces every drawn glyph with an inline path and
Ghostscript emits no reusable form for them, so a fifty-page document grows
from half a megabyte to thirty-three and takes about half a second per page.
Most jobs are nowhere near the printer's limit, so the cost of each embedded
font program and the glyphs drawn from it are estimated first and cheap jobs
are relayed untouched, which is both free and perfectly faithful. Measured on a
Color LaserJet Pro MFP M283fdw, ordinary browser jobs estimate between 1200 and
1900 and print, while the lowest observed failure estimates about 4000. A file
whose cost cannot be determined is always converted, never assumed cheap.
.TP
.B \-\-max\-pdf\-bytes " \fIMB\fR"
Rasterise rather than send an outlined PDF larger than this. Default 60.
Overridden by the printer's own
.B pdf\-k\-octets\-supported
where it reports one, so the shipped default only applies to a device that
declares no limit.
.TP
.B \-\-all\-formats
Offer clients every document format the printer supports, including
PostScript.
.IP
PostScript is withheld by default. It is interpreted by exactly the task that
fails on these devices, and it cannot be converted the way PDF is: handing
PostScript to Ghostscript means running its PostScript interpreter, which this
program is careful never to do. Offering it would advertise a repaired queue
that silently is not one. PCL and PCL\-XL are offered either way \(em they are
a separate interpreter on the device, and are relayed unchanged.
.TP
.B \-\-fail\-closed
Reject a PDF that cannot be converted, instead of forwarding it unchanged.
By default a conversion failure relays the original, on the grounds that a job
which might not print beats one that prints wrongly. That is the safe choice
for printing and the unsafe one for the guarantee this proxy exists to give,
because whoever sends the document also decides whether conversion fails. This
option inverts that trade.
.TP
.B \-\-archive\-max\-bytes " \fIMB\fR"
Total size cap for the archive, in megabytes. Default 512. A count alone is not
a bound when the documents are chosen by whoever is printing.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-advertise
Do not publish the queues over DNS\-SD. Useful when discovery is handled
elsewhere.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-convert
Relay jobs untouched. Intended for comparison against a converting instance,
to confirm that conversion is what makes the difference.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-port " \fIPORT\fR"
Port to listen on. Default 631, which requires
.B CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE
or an adjusted
.IR net.ipv4.ip_unprivileged_port_start .
.TP
.B \-\-timeout " \fISECONDS\fR"
Time allowed for each conversion and each request to the printer. Default 300.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Log protocol detail.

.SH CONVERSION
A job passes through up to three stages, stopping at the first that suffices.
.TP
.B Relayed untouched
The estimated font cost is at or below
.BR \-\-convert\-threshold .
Most jobs land here: it costs nothing and is perfectly faithful.
.TP
.B Text outlined
Glyphs become filled paths, so no font program reaches the printer. Text stays
vector, so the printer's own rasteriser still renders it at full device
precision.
.TP
.B Rasterised
Only when the outlined form would exceed what the printer will accept as a PDF.
These devices advertise
.B pdf\-k\-octets\-supported
of 0\-75000, and outlining inlines an outline at every glyph occurrence, so a
long document can pass that. The page is then sent as 600 dpi contone URF.
Fidelity is lower, because the geometry is committed to the device grid before
the printer sees it, but the printer still applies its own halftoning and edge
enhancement, and the job prints rather than being rejected. The job's document
format is updated to match.
.PP
The converter reads three environment variables, which the service unit may
set:
.BR MAX_PDF_BYTES " (default 60000000),"
.BR RASTER_DPI " (600), and"
.B RASTER_COLORSPACE
(19 for sRGB; 18 for 8\-bit grey, which halves the size and avoids
composite\-black fringing on text).

.SH CALIBRATION
.B The default threshold of 2500 is calibrated against one printer.
It was measured on a Color LaserJet Pro MFP M283fdw by bisection: estimates up
to 3558 printed, 3697 and above did not. 2500 leaves roughly a thirty per cent
margin, chosen because the limit shifts with the typeface -- one face failed at
520 drawn glyphs where another survived 527.
.PP
Another model will have a different limit, and this one is not new. If jobs
still vanish, lower the threshold; if jobs are being converted that need not
be, raise it.
.B \-\-convert\-threshold 0
converts everything, which is the safe setting when the limit is unknown, at
the cost of doing expensive work on every job.
.PP
To measure a printer directly, note that a job which exceeds the budget marks
no paper, so only the passing probes cost anything.
.PP
The device's own page counter is the reliable signal, because an affected
printer reports the job completed either way and every layer above it repeats
that. The Printer MIB (RFC 3805) exposes it on every network printer worth the
name:
.PP
.RS
.nf
snmpget \-v2c \-c public PRINTER 1.3.6.1.2.1.43.10.2.1.4.1.1
.fi
.RE
.PP
Manufacturers usually expose more than the standard MIB, sometimes including
the interpreter's own error log \(em on the printer this was developed against,
an assertion naming
.B fontcache.c
is what identified the defect. Those paths are vendor specific and this program
neither uses nor depends on them; look for an event log or diagnostics page in
the printer's web interface, or a printable event\-log report on its panel.
.PP
Print documents of increasing font cost, read the counter before and after
each, and bisect between the largest that printed and the smallest that did
not. Set the threshold comfortably below the boundary found.

.SH BEHAVIOUR
Both plaintext IPP and implicit TLS are accepted on the same port, and both
IPv4 and IPv6 are served.
.PP
Printer attributes are mirrored from the real device on every request. Only
identity and URIs are rewritten \(em the queue name, its UUID, the URIs clients
should use, and the locations of the printer's icons and localised strings,
which are re\-served by this daemon because clients may have no route to the
printer's own web server. Capabilities and status pass through unchanged, so
duplex, trays, media, colour modes and live supply and error state are always
correct without configuration.
.PP
Documents that are not PDF are relayed untouched, as are PDFs whose conversion
fails or leaves any font program behind. A job that might not print is
preferable to one that prints something wrong.
.PP
Jobs are forwarded one at a time, because affected printers report
.B multiple\-document\-jobs\-supported
as false and mishandle a second job arriving mid\-transfer.

.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/local/lib/ippfix/
Installation directory.
.TP
.I /usr/local/lib/ippfix/defont
PDF conversion helper. May be run by hand for diagnosis.
.TP
.I /etc/ippfix/ippfix.crt\fR, \fI/etc/ippfix/ippfix.key
Self\-signed TLS credentials created by the installer.
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/ippfix.service
Service unit; edit
.B ExecStart
to configure the printers.

.SH EXAMPLES
Serve one printer:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ippfix ipp://printer.example/ipp/print
.fi
.RE
.PP
Serve two, under explicit names:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ippfix upstairs=ipp://printer1.example/ipp/print \\
       downstairs=ipp://printer2.example/ipp/print
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run a second, non\-converting instance for comparison:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ippfix \-\-port 6310 \-\-no\-advertise \-\-no\-convert \\
       ipp://printer.example/ipp/print
.fi
.RE

.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Each job is logged with its queue, operation, and the result of conversion:
.PP
.RS
.nf
upstairs   Print\-Job   HTTP 200  [outlined 393836 \-> 76789 bytes in 0.2s]
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B relayed
indicates the job was passed through unchanged.
.PP
Because an affected printer reports success whether or not it printed, the
reliable check is the device's own total impression count, which does not
advance for a job that died. On HP devices both that counter and the assertion
log are readable over HTTP from
.I /DevMgmt/ProductUsageDyn.xml
and
.IR /DevMgmt/ProductLogsDyn.xml .

.SH NOTES
If clients can also reach the printers directly, they will see both those and
the proxy queues, and users will choose arbitrarily between them. Suppress the
printers' own
.BR _ipp ,
.B _ipps
and
.B _pdl\-datastream
advertisements, but leave
.B _uscan
and
.B _uscans
alone or scanning will stop working.
.PP
Only one mDNS responder may hold UDP port 5353. With
.B systemd\-resolved
present, set
.B MulticastDNS=no
in
.I /etc/systemd/resolved.conf
and restart it, or run with
.BR \-\-no\-advertise .

